        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "s3-f1-alternative-registry",
      "boundaryId": "s3-dependency-rule",
      "criterionIds": [
        "DEP-1"
      ],
      "descriptionMarkdown": "**Expected:** DEP-1 accepts crates.io only (plus pinned public git); a dependency a clean clone cannot fetch must be rejected. **Observed:** a dependency from an alternative (private/other) registry passes both gates. `dependency_violations` never looks at the `registry` / `registry-index` keys, and `source_violation` returns `None` for any source starting with `registry+` (`tests/dependency_sources.rs`, `source_violation`), so `foo = { version = \"1\", registry = \"internal\" }` and a Cargo.lock/`cargo metadata` source `registry+https://private.example/index` are accepted. (Verified by reading the code paths; the previous `package_layout` test had the same `starts_with(\"registry+\")` looseness, but DEP-1 and the DEP-2 rule text say crates.io.)\n\n**Repair:** (1) in `dependency_violations` push a violation when `spec` contains `registry` or `registry-index` (the same for `[patch]` entries, which go through it); (2) in `source_violation` accept only exactly `registry+https://github.com/rust-lang/crates.io-index` (what cargo prints for crates.io, including with the sparse protocol; the real lockfile already uses it) and reject any other `registry+`/`sparse+` source; (3) add inline reject cases: `registry = \"internal\"`, `registry-index = \"https://example.com/index\"` in `[dependencies]` and in a `[patch.crates-io]` entry, and a `source_violation(\"a\", \"registry+https://private.example/index\")` assertion.",
      "severity": "major",
      "status": "resolved",
      "regressionRefs": [
        {
          "repositoryId": "rayx-cli",
          "path": "tests/dependency_sources.rs",
          "symbol": "alternative_registries_are_rejected"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "s3-f2-rev-must-be-a-commit",
      "boundaryId": "s3-dependency-rule",
      "criterionIds": [
        "DEP-1"
      ],
      "descriptionMarkdown": "**Expected:** git dependencies are pinned, not selected by a moving ref (DEP-1: reject `branch`, `tag` or nothing). **Observed:** `rev` is only checked for presence, so `gpux = { git = \"https://github.com/rayx-ui/Gpux\", rev = \"main\" }` (or `rev = \"refs/heads/main\"`/a tag name; cargo accepts any ref in `rev`) is accepted; the lockfile source is then `?rev=main#<sha>`, which `source_violation` also accepts via `contains(\"?rev=\")`. The build is only as reproducible as the lock file, contradicting the rule text 'pinned to a `rev`'.\n\n**Repair:** in `dependency_violations` require the `rev` value to be a string of 7-40 hex digits (`rev.len() in 7..=40 && all ascii hexdigit`), pushing a violation such as 'rev must be a commit hash' otherwise (also for `[patch]` entries), and make `source_violation` check that the text between `?rev=` and `#` is hex the same way. Add reject cases `rev = \"main\"` and `rev = \"refs/heads/main\"` to `git_dependencies_must_be_pinned_by_rev` and a `git+...?rev=main#0123` case to `resolved_sources_are_registry_or_pinned_public_git`; the existing accept samples use `rev = \"abc\"`/`\"a\"`, so lengthen them to real-looking hashes.",
